"""Cyber Academy technical lessons undergoing substantive editorial review."""
COURSE = {
  "key": "academy-check-v1",
  "title": "Evidence, Logs & Detection Fundamentals",
  "academy_id": "university",
  "pathway": "university",
  "stage": "check",
  "revision": "v1",
  "description": "Cyber Academy check course on technical controls, interpretation of evidence, practical tests and defensible reasoning.",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "title": "Event sources and questions",
      "position": 1,
      "content": "**Learning objectives.** Select an event source for a specific question, identify the fields needed to interpret it, and separate a recorded action from an unsupported incident conclusion. Discover introduced logs as bounded observations. This course checks your reasoning with supplied fictional records. It requires no access to a live system, private account or external evidence packet.\n\n**Begin with a question that can be answered.** “What happened?” is too broad for choosing a source. “Did account A12 request a report?” differs from “Did the service return report data?” and “Was that disclosure permitted?” A gateway can record receipt of a request. The application may record the decision and outcome. Its permission records may establish the policy applicable at that time. An event source is useful when its observation matches the question, not simply because it produces many rows or has a security-related name.\n\nWorked example: A fictional journal service supplies three records for request Q41. At 12:00:01Z the gateway records a report request from account A12. At 12:00:02Z the application records “report access denied, subscription inactive”. At 12:00:02Z the gateway records HTTP 403 and a 480-byte response. An alert labels the request “report downloaded”. The records support receipt and denial under the application's stated decision. The small response could be an error page; its byte count does not establish that the requested report was delivered.\n\n**Read the event's definition.** “Success” may mean that a connection opened, authentication passed, a job entered a queue or a database transaction committed. Ask which stage the source records. A transport-level success can coexist with application refusal. A label assigned by a detection rule is an interpretation built from source events, not an independent observation. In Q41, the alert may have mistaken every response for a completed download. Compare its claim with the underlying gateway and application definitions before counting an incident.\n\n**A minimal source map.** For receipt, use the gateway's request reference, event time, route and outcome. For the access decision, use the application's account reference, resource reference, decision and relevant reason. For permission, use the subscription or entitlement state effective at the event time. Keep raw passwords, session secrets and complete report content out of routine logs. An account reference helps correlation without proving who physically operated the device. Different sources may share one upstream record, so apparent agreement is not always independent corroboration.\n\n**Check what could be missing.** The application might record refusals but omit successful responses, or a collector might drop one event category. A silent source is not proof that no activity occurred. Document the source's coverage, collection health and retention window before using absence as evidence. Conversely, missing detail should not be filled by inventing a successful download. The appropriate conclusion can remain narrow while a technical owner confirms the event definition or collection gap.\n\n**Source-selection exercise.** Using Q41 only, answer five questions: was a request received; which account context was recorded; what decision did the application report; was report content disclosed; and who used the device? Name the best supplied source for each answer and mark unresolved questions. Then propose one additional evidence item that could help verify disclosure without collecting every report the account has ever accessed. Avoid changing the scenario by assuming a second hidden request.\n\n**Explained answer.** Receipt, account context and recorded refusal are supported. The supplied records do not independently establish report-content disclosure or human identity. A controlled review of the response's content type, application outcome and relevant delivery evidence could clarify disclosure within an authorised scope. It is inappropriate to infer theft from the alert's label or to pronounce the account harmless for all time. Reflection: Which source definition would you challenge first? The takeaway is a disciplined link from question to source, fields and remaining limitation, with the alert treated as a lead rather than a verdict."
    },
    {
      "title": "Time integrity and event ordering",
      "position": 2,
      "content": "**Learning objectives.** Normalise explicit time offsets, distinguish event time from collection time, and explain an ordering that remains uncertain because a clock was not trustworthy. Precise-looking timestamps can produce a persuasive but false story. Checking temporal relationships is therefore part of reasoning about evidence, not merely a formatting step before sorting a spreadsheet.\n\n**Preserve the original value.** An event timestamp may use UTC, a stated local offset or an unqualified local time. The suffix Z denotes UTC in the examples here. A positive offset means the displayed local time is ahead of UTC: subtract the offset to convert to UTC. Keep both the original value and the conversion. Changing the recorded source time without retaining the original hides the transformation and makes another reader unable to check your work. Date changes matter when subtracting an offset crosses midnight.\n\nWorked example: A fictional document service provides four cards for 2026-10-31. Card A records a request at 13:00:04Z, received by the collector at 13:00:06Z. Card B records processing at 14:00:05+01:00, collected at 13:00:20Z. Card C records a notification at 13:00:09Z, collected at 13:00:10Z. Card D records “14:00:03” with no timezone and an unverified device clock. Treat all supplied dates as the same calendar date unless a conversion says otherwise. No additional records are available.\n\n**Work through the conversions.** B converts to 13:00:05Z. The stated event times place A, then B, then C, assuming their clocks are sufficiently aligned for that precision. The collection order is A, C, then B. A delayed pipeline explains why sorting by arrival would produce a different sequence. B's fifteen-second interval from event time to collection is an observed timestamp difference; it is not automatically pure network transit time because buffering, processing and clock error can contribute. State that qualification instead of assigning the delay to a specific component without evidence.\n\n**Uncertainty is not solved by choosing a convenient offset.** D could appear early or late depending on its timezone and clock accuracy. Do not assume UTC+1 simply because another source uses it. Obtain the device's time configuration and relevant clock-quality evidence from its owner if the real investigation requires precise ordering. Even with an offset, clock drift can make subsecond or near-adjacent ordering unreliable. A timestamp describes what the source reported, not an independent guarantee that the source's clock was correct.\n\n**Identifiers can constrain order differently.** A system may record a request's accepted state before its completion state by design, while collection reverses their arrival. That relationship can support a partial order for the same validated request reference. It cannot fix every unrelated timestamp or establish how long the operation actually took. Explain whether your ordering depends on clock alignment, a documented application sequence or both. A reused request reference or a retry can also complicate interpretation, so provenance and event meaning still matter.\n\n**Timeline exercise.** Write a table for A–D showing original time, normalised value where justified, collection time and confidence in ordering. Then convert a fifth example, 2026-11-01T00:10:00+02:00, to UTC. State the date as well as the time. Finally write one sentence that distinguishes the supported A–B–C event-time sequence from the unresolved position of D. The exercise tests interpretation, not your ability to supply a missing timezone from imagination.\n\n**Answer discussion.** The fifth value converts to 2026-10-31T22:10:00Z. A–B–C is the order of the reported normalised event times, qualified by clock alignment. D has no justified UTC placement. Reflection: Would collecting every source faster resolve D's problem? Faster transport would not establish its offset or correct its clock. The takeaway is a timeline with traceable conversions, separate collection observations and visible uncertainty, rather than a single sorted column presented as an unquestionable sequence of events."
    },
    {
      "title": "Identity traces and session context",
      "position": 3,
      "content": "**Learning objectives.** Distinguish account context from a human actor, connect authentication to later session activity carefully, and assess whether changing an IP address proves a different person used an account. Identity evidence is often informative without being conclusive. This lesson asks you to combine supplied observations while resisting both premature accusations and unjustified reassurance.\n\n**The recorded principal has a boundary.** A principal is an identity under which a system evaluates an action. An authentication service can record that credentials or another accepted proof were supplied for an account. A session allows later activity under that identity according to the service's rules. The application may record a session reference rather than repeat the entire authentication event. These records concern service decisions. They do not directly observe the person's hands, the safety of the endpoint or the reason for an action.\n\nWorked example: A fictional research wiki records account R8 authenticating at 15:20Z using approved additional verification and establishing session S22. At 15:24Z, S22 reads a project page through network address N1. At 15:27Z, the same session edits the page through N2. R8's owner reports switching from campus Wi-Fi to mobile data at approximately 15:25Z and says they edited the page. The address change is consistent with that report, but the records do not make the explanation certain or prove that only one person could use the session.\n\n**Use context with its limitations.** IP addresses identify network communication context, not reliably a unique person. Shared gateways can place many users behind one public address. A network change can give one user a different address. Approximate location data can describe provider infrastructure rather than the user's exact location. Device labels may be self-reported or incomplete. These facts do not make identity logs useless; they explain why a conclusion should use several relevant observations and describe the strength of each link.\n\n**A session reference is evidence only if its handling is understood.** S22 supports a relationship between the recorded requests if the application genuinely assigns and propagates the reference as described. Reuse, truncation or a logging bug could weaken that relationship. A session identifier printed in a classroom exercise should be a harmless invented label, not a copied live token. Actual session secrets should not be placed in public reports. Correlation should preserve useful references while avoiding the creation of another route to account access.\n\n**Compare hypotheses that predict different observations.** One hypothesis is the owner's reported network switch and edit. Another is that somebody else obtained access to the session or device. Evidence about the edit content, the owner's precise confirmation and authorised endpoint records could help distinguish them. A successful additional-verification event at 15:20 does not automatically authenticate every later physical action; an already established session may continue without another prompt. Conversely, changing networks alone does not establish session theft. State which evidence would challenge each hypothesis.\n\n**Identity reasoning exercise.** Write three findings from the R8 pack: the identity context accepted by the service; the operations recorded under S22; and your confidence about the human actor. Then add one changed card: the owner confirms the page read but denies the edit. Update the assessment without claiming that denial alone proves a particular attacker. Choose a narrowly relevant follow-up question and explain why it matters more than merely collecting more approximate geolocation screenshots.\n\n**Explained answer.** The service accepted R8's authentication and recorded a read and edit under S22. The original owner report corroborates the legitimate-use hypothesis within its stated accuracy. The changed card creates a meaningful discrepancy requiring further review of the edit, session and endpoint context. It still does not identify another person. Reflection: What conclusion can you state firmly without human attribution? The takeaway is to report system identity, session continuity and person-level confidence separately. That structure supports later investigations without converting every account event into an accusation against its owner."
    },
    {
      "title": "Network flows and their limitations",
      "position": 4,
      "content": "**Learning objectives.** Interpret network flow attributes, compare an observation with a relevant baseline, and explain why connection volume alone cannot establish data theft or legitimate purpose. A flow record summarises communication under a particular collection method. It may include endpoints, ports, timing and byte counts, but often not the application content or the human reason for the exchange.\n\n**Know the measurement.** Ask whether a byte count represents sent traffic, received traffic or both, and whether the observation is one connection or an aggregation. Check which observation point produced it. Traffic passing through a shared gateway may require additional mapping before it can be associated with a specific internal workload. A destination port can suggest a protocol but does not prove the application intent. Encryption can prevent a network observer from seeing content while leaving some communication metadata visible; it does not make every other evidence source unavailable.\n\nWorked example: A fictional campus graphics server communicates with an external software distribution service between 02:00 and 02:18. The supplied flow record reports 6 GB received and 90 MB sent. An approved maintenance note schedules an update at 02:00. The server's update record reports package retrieval and installation during that interval. A chart highlights “large external transfer” in red. The flow is consistent with a substantial download during maintenance, not direct proof that 6 GB of internal data left the server.\n\n**Direction changes the claim.** Received bytes and sent bytes are different observations. Some outgoing traffic may be requests, acknowledgements or other protocol overhead, but do not assign all 90 MB to a specific cause without appropriate evidence. Likewise, the recorded 6 GB may not equal the size of a single installed package because aggregation or retry behaviour can matter. The supplied maintenance and update records make a legitimate explanation plausible. They do not establish that every connection in the interval was authorised or that the distribution destination could never be misused.\n\n**A baseline needs comparable conditions.** A normal teaching day without software updates is a weak comparison for a scheduled update window. Compare relevant historical maintenance intervals, endpoint function and expected destination, if available. A first-time update can still be legitimate; an unusual event merits explanation rather than automatic classification as malicious. Small transfers can also matter when their content or destination is inappropriate. Volume is a clue whose value depends on the service context and the question being asked.\n\n**Seek corroboration without inventing content.** The update record supports package retrieval, while destination ownership and deployment records can help establish expected use. Endpoint process evidence might link the network communication to the approved updater, if available through authorised investigation. A flow alone generally cannot name the file contents or determine whether an employee intended disclosure. Do not try to decrypt or intercept other people's traffic as a classroom exercise. State which missing evidence limits the conclusion and who could legitimately provide it.\n\n**Flow interpretation exercise.** From the supplied pack, write a note with three headings: observed communication; supported explanation; unresolved scope. Then change the record to show 6 GB sent and 90 MB received while keeping the same maintenance note. Explain why the changed direction warrants a different question. It does not prove theft, but it weakens the specific explanation that the large volume was mainly a software download. Identify one evidence item that could resolve the mismatch.\n\n**Answer discussion.** The original evidence supports a bounded statement that the observed communication aligns with the approved update period and recorded retrieval. The changed pack needs review of the responsible process, aggregation meaning and actual activity; a maintenance label cannot explain every direction or amount automatically. Reflection: Which field would you verify before quoting the chart's transfer total? Your takeaway is accurate interpretation of direction, aggregation and context, with a measured conclusion about what network metadata can show. Later investigations combine those observations with other sources; this lesson establishes the limits of the flow evidence itself."
    },
    {
      "title": "Detection rules and false positives",
      "position": 5,
      "content": "**Learning objectives.** Distinguish matching a rule from confirming an incident, calculate simple detection measures from a labelled exercise set, and explain why reducing alert volume may increase missed events. Rules are decision aids built from observable conditions. Their usefulness depends on the question they address, the evidence available and the cost of both unnecessary investigations and failures to notice consequential activity.\n\n**Separate two meanings of positive.** A rule may correctly identify that an administrator signed in outside normal hours, while the activity is authorised maintenance. That is a valid condition match but may be a false positive relative to a goal of finding unauthorised activity. Define the target outcome before calling an alert true or false. Otherwise two analysts can argue over the same label while assessing different things. An unusual action is not automatically malicious, and a known maintenance window is not blanket permission for every action inside it.\n\nWorked example: A fictional exercise contains twenty labelled administrator sessions. Six are classified as unauthorised under the exercise's stated permissions; fourteen are legitimate. An after-hours rule alerts on four of the six unauthorised sessions and six of the fourteen legitimate sessions. There are ten alerts: four useful detections and six unnecessary investigations for this defined target. Two unauthorised sessions are missed and eight legitimate sessions are not alerted. These labels are supplied exercise truth, not claims about an unseen real dataset.\n\n**Calculate and interpret.** Precision is the proportion of alerts that correspond to the target condition: four divided by ten, or 40 percent. Recall is the proportion of target events detected: four divided by six, approximately 66.7 percent. The false-positive rate is six divided by fourteen, approximately 42.9 percent. It is not six divided by ten; that would describe the fraction of alerts that are false under these labels. Each denominator answers a different question. Small exercise counts are useful for learning arithmetic but cannot establish performance across a university's real activity.\n\n**Context can help or create a blind spot.** A maintenance reference, assigned role and approved task can help prioritise review. Automatically suppressing every session with a ticket number may hide misuse of a valid reference. A narrowly tuned rule may fit yesterday's examples while missing a new pattern. Compare changes against both target and legitimate cases, including examples not used to choose the rule. A reduction from ten alerts to three could be an improvement or a damaging loss of visibility; examine which labelled cases moved, not only the count.\n\n**Operational cost belongs in the assessment.** Responders have limited attention. Six unnecessary investigations can delay assessment of the four useful detections. The consequence depends on how quickly triage distinguishes them and what harm a delayed response permits. Conversely, a missed low-consequence event and a missed privileged destructive action need not have the same impact. Simple metrics are helpful summaries, not a complete prioritisation policy. State the rule's coverage and its response purpose alongside the arithmetic.\n\n**Detection exercise.** Recalculate precision and recall if a proposed change produces five alerts: three unauthorised sessions and two legitimate sessions. Keep the same twenty-session population and six unauthorised labels. Decide whether you would adopt the change based only on the improved precision. Then identify a further question about the three newly missed unauthorised sessions that a responsible reviewer should ask before deciding.\n\n**Explained answer.** Precision becomes three fifths, or 60 percent, while recall falls to three sixths, or 50 percent. Improved precision alone does not settle the trade-off. Determine which harmful behaviours are missed, whether other controls cover them and whether triage capacity genuinely improves. Reflection: How would your conclusion change if the supplied labels were uncertain? Verify labelling quality and report that limitation rather than treating disputed truth as certain. The takeaway is explicit definitions and denominators, tested against both target and normal behaviour, with alert reduction never mistaken automatically for risk reduction."
    },
    {
      "title": "Evidence preservation and privacy",
      "position": 6,
      "content": "**Learning objectives.** Preserve a record's provenance, create a limited-purpose derived copy without changing the underlying finding, and explain why a checksum does not establish that the original account of events was true. Evidence handling protects both investigative usefulness and people whose information appears in records. This lesson uses a fictional support bundle, so no private files or real credentials need to be collected.\n\n**Start with source and purpose.** Record what produced an item, when and how it was obtained, which scope it covers and who is authorised to use it. A support export can contain useful request references alongside unrelated personal fields. Having permission to investigate one failure does not imply permission to distribute every field to every audience. Agree the question and minimise collection where possible. Retention and sharing must follow the organisation's applicable requirements; this course supplies no universal legal retention period.\n\nWorked example: A fictional image-processing service provides an authorised diagnostic bundle for a failed thumbnail task. It includes event time, request K72, component name, status and file-owner email addresses. It also accidentally includes a live-looking access token in a debug field. The classroom exercise replaces that token with the inert label TOKEN-REMOVED. A public bug report needs the error category and request sequence, not personal addresses or an authentication secret. The authorised technical team handles any actual secret exposure through its own security process.\n\n**Keep original and derived purposes distinct.** Where authorised and required, retain the original in controlled storage under the organisation's evidence process. Create a separate redacted copy for the narrower reporting purpose, documenting which fields were removed or replaced. Do not silently edit the controlled original to make it easier to share. Redaction should remove information rather than merely cover text visually while leaving it recoverable underneath. A derived copy should retain enough timing and correlation context to support the question, or its reduced evidential value must be stated.\n\n**Understand the integrity check.** A cryptographic checksum can help detect a change relative to a previously recorded trusted value. It does not prove that the source system's clock was correct, that its account mapping was accurate or that the export was complete when captured. A hash of a fabricated record still faithfully identifies that fabricated file. Provenance, access control and documentation of transformations remain necessary. Copying a checksum beside a file without protecting either from convenient alteration also weakens the usefulness of the comparison.\n\n**Different audiences need different detail.** The component maintainer may need restricted technical context to reproduce the failure. A service owner may need the effect, uncertainty and next action. A public issue should contain only what is appropriate for publication. Those versions should not contradict each other: a redacted summary cannot invent certainty absent from the source. If removing the account field prevents assessment of an identity-related hypothesis, state that the public version cannot independently support that part of the finding and direct authorised review to the controlled evidence.\n\n**Handling exercise.** From the supplied bundle fields, choose what to include in a restricted reproduction note and what to include publicly. Decide whether request K72 can remain as an invented correlation reference. Write a transformation note explaining the removed emails and token field. Then evaluate two claims: “the matching checksum proves nobody ever altered the event” and “the redacted copy is sufficient to analyse the error sequence but not identify a person”. Explain the evidence boundaries in both.\n\n**Answer discussion.** The checksum supports consistency with a particular recorded file value, not the entire history or truth of the event. The second statement can be appropriate if the retained fields actually preserve the relevant sequence and their clocks are understood. The secret-like field should not be copied into the public report; actual exposure may require additional action beyond redaction. Reflection: What would invalidate your derived copy for the stated purpose? Removing every useful request reference or changing event order could make the analysis unreproducible. The takeaway is controlled provenance, meaningful minimisation and honest limits, with integrity tools supporting rather than replacing a trustworthy handling process."
    },
    {
      "title": "Correlating a modest incident",
      "position": 7,
      "content": "**Learning objectives.** Join a small set of records using justified references, distinguish correlation from cause, and write a conclusion that separates the recorded action from human attribution. You will use the supplied evidence only. The later guided-investigation course expands scope and collection; here the challenge is to reason correctly about a modest case without inventing records that make the story more complete.\n\n**The reported concern.** A fictional student project board changes task T18's assignee from M2 to M9. M2 says they did not request the change. The board team supplies five synthetic records, all with verified UTC times for this exercise. At 16:10:00Z, identity service event I6 authenticates coordinator account C3 and establishes session S5. At 16:12:10Z, the application records request R80 under C3/S5 to change T18's assignee. At 16:12:11Z, the audit records commit J4 for T18, with R80 and the old and new assignee references.\n\nWorked example: A gateway entry at 16:12:10Z also records R80 reaching the board application. A separate request R81 at 16:12:10Z reads a different task under learner M4. Matching R81 solely because its timestamp is close would connect the wrong activity to the assignee change. R80 is the stronger link between the gateway, application request and commit, provided the references have the documented meaning. The supplied policy says coordinators may reassign tasks within projects assigned to them; C3's project assignment is not included in the pack.\n\n**What the records establish.** The application attributes the change request to C3/S5, and the commit record confirms a change to T18 in that request path. The gateway corroborates receipt of R80 but does not independently verify the permission decision. The identity event supports the session's account context. It does not prove which person physically issued R80. The evidence supports an observed assignment change, but current policy compliance remains unresolved because C3's relevant project scope at the time is missing.\n\n**Keep competing explanations testable.** An authorised coordinator may have reassigned the task for ordinary project management. An account acting outside its project scope may indicate a permission failure. A person other than the rightful coordinator may have used the session. These explanations do not all require the same next evidence. The effective project assignment and decision record help with authority; the coordinator's confirmation and authorised session context help with attribution. M2's denial matters but does not settle the case, because the policy permits some changes without M2 initiating them.\n\n**A join needs provenance.** Request references can be duplicated, truncated or generated separately by different components. Verify their intended uniqueness and propagation before treating a matching string as conclusive. In this exercise R80 is stated to identify the same request path; in a real case that needs validation. Do not hide the difference between a directly recorded association and an inference based on timing. A diagram can use solid links for supplied references and labelled uncertain links for hypotheses, keeping the reader from mistaking an attractive chain for proven causation.\n\n**Correlation exercise.** Build a five-row evidence table naming each record's source, relevant reference and supported proposition. Mark R81 as unrelated to the assignee-change path under the supplied facts. Write a three-sentence assessment covering the confirmed action, recorded principal and unresolved authority. Then choose between requesting the entire university's browsing history and obtaining C3's project assignment effective at 16:12. Explain which is better aligned with the unanswered policy question.\n\n**Explained answer.** The narrow project-assignment record is more relevant and proportionate to the authority question. If it shows C3 assigned to this project, that supports policy compliance but still does not establish physical authorship. If it excludes the project, investigate why the application permitted the change and preserve the distinction between account authority and human motive. Reflection: Which finding survives either outcome? T18's assignee changed through the recorded R80 path. The takeaway is a modest, defensible correlation with explicit joins and unanswered questions, not a complete incident narrative manufactured from adjacent timestamps."
    },
    {
      "title": "Detection debrief and readiness",
      "position": 8,
      "content": "**Learning objectives.** Evaluate a bounded detection rehearsal, separate collection, interpretation and notification outcomes, and define a retest that addresses the actual gap. The preceding lessons supplied source selection, timing, identity, flows, rule metrics, handling and correlation. This final check combines them in a paper debrief rather than assuming that a green dashboard demonstrates monitoring readiness.\n\n**State the tested claim.** A fictional research archive wants notification when its application records an unauthorised document-sharing attempt. The agreed synthetic rehearsal uses a test account and invented document. It should produce a refusal event, preserve useful request context and notify the designated responder. These are separate observations. The exercise does not claim to cover every archive threat, every application instance or a real data disclosure. Define those boundaries before interpreting whether the test succeeded.\n\nWorked example: Test request V7 is refused at 09:30:00Z. The application emits event category SHARE_DENIED with request V7 and a harmless test identity reference. Collection receives the event at 09:30:02Z. A dashboard shows the source as connected, but the rule expects category SHARING_BLOCKED. No alert is created. A separate channel test message arrives at the responder at 09:31Z. That message confirms the communication channel can deliver a test notification; it does not establish that the application event was recognised or that V7 triggered a usable alert.\n\n**Locate the failure precisely.** The supplied evidence supports refusal and collection for V7. The category mismatch explains why the stated rule did not recognise this event under the given configuration. There is no basis to report that the source was entirely absent or that all response communication failed. Likewise, the connected-source indicator is too broad to prove event correctness. The useful finding names the event contract between source and rule, the missed outcome and the relevant configuration owner. This is more actionable than recommending “more monitoring”.\n\n**Keep correction and verification distinct.** Editing the rule's category may be the appropriate correction, but its completion is not a successful retest. Repeat a tagged synthetic request through the normal source path, observe collection and rule evaluation, and record notification and acknowledgement. Also include a legitimate sharing event that should not produce this refusal alert. A rule changed to alert on every event could pass the refused-request example while creating excessive noise. The meaning of source fields and the expected alert remain part of the acceptance criteria.\n\n**Write a bounded debrief.** Record the claim, test conditions, expected observations, actual evidence, defect, owner and retest condition. Preserve the failed result beside later results so the improvement is visible. If the new test succeeds on one archive instance, say so; do not silently extend it to an untested second application using a different schema. A changed event name in a later deployment should prompt revalidation of the source-to-rule contract. Detection maintenance includes change awareness, not only adding more alert rules.\n\n**Readiness exercise.** Give each V7 stage a status: observed success, observed failure or not assessed. Include source refusal, collector receipt, rule match, alert notification from V7 and human response to an actual V7 alert. Treat the separate channel message as its own result. Then propose a two-case retest and write a conclusion that neither discards the valid collection evidence nor claims the full detection objective worked.\n\n**Answer discussion.** Refusal and collection succeeded in the supplied run; rule matching failed. No V7 alert notification occurred, and response to such an alert was not exercised. The channel-only message proves a narrower delivery capability. A meaningful retest includes the corrected refused event and a permitted-sharing comparison, with observed alert handling for the former. Reflection: Which record would show the test still bypassed the real source? A manually injected channel message would not supply the application-to-rule evidence. Your takeaway is readiness described through observed stages and scope, with improvements closed by a relevant retest.\n\nFurther reading: OWASP Logging Cheat Sheet, https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Logging_Cheat_Sheet.html — source selection, event attributes, exclusions, verification and protection. RFC Editor, RFC 3339, https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3339 — timestamp representation and offsets; its publication page notes later updates. Both official pages were opened and relevance checked on 31 October 2026. They are optional references rather than additional evidence for the fictional exercises."
    }
  ]
}
